#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""bm-eclipse-doctor — Eclipse compile-error triage and repair, driven by PDE facts.

One call, no conversational round-trip: --apply repairs everything mechanically
repairable and only hands back real code errors.

Every diagnostic pass is ONE read (`doctor_snapshot`: workspace_info + get_problems +
list_projects + get_bundle_state + unresolvedTypes) and every workspace remedy of that
pass goes through ONE `apply_workspace_batch` (delete + open + import + refresh + clean,
auto-build suspended, a single build). Maven rebuilds go through ONE reactor.

Classification is by FACT, not by symptom or by message shape:

  metadataInvisible non-empty (PDE metadata on disk, invisible to Eclipse) -> refresh
  not in the workspace, .project on disk                                   -> import
  closed                                                                   -> open (transitive)
  declared source folder missing/empty                                     -> maven rebuild
  resolved, yet the dependent still has markers                            -> clean the dependent
  neither in the workspace nor on disk                                     -> reload_target_platform
  unresolved type JDT knows about (jdt-visible)                            -> clean the dependent
  unresolved type only on disk (disk-only)                                 -> refresh+clean supplier
  unresolved type nowhere, in generated sources, untracked                 -> delete + clean
  none of the above                                                        -> real code error, hand off

The three type-level facts come from the plugin (`unresolvedTypes`), which asks the JDT
model and the disk; the script never parses a message to get a missing name — markers
carry `problemKind` and `unresolvedName` as structured attributes.

Stale GWT codegen is invisible to PDE (both bundles stay resolved; the error is a JDT
error inside generated sources). Pairing has three states: `paired` with the freshness
signal (rebuild), `paired` without it (real doubt -> report), and `no-counterpart` (the
question does not apply -> never report-only, either an orphan codegen output or a
hand-off).

Traceability: [doctor:decision] / [doctor:outcome] / [doctor:pass] / [doctor:handoff]
lines on stdout tie each remedy to its effect, and the arithmetic is verifiable: each
project's delta is attributed to exactly one remedy. Nothing is ever written to disk
unless asked (--report-out).

Usage:
  bm-eclipse-doctor [--apply] [--sync] [--fix-gwt] [--report] [--report-all-facts]
                    [--report-out PATH] [--severity error|warning|all] [--json]
                    [--summary] [bundle-hint] [--config PATH]
                    [--open NAME[,NAME...]] [--close NAME[,NAME...]]
                    [--open-group {gwt,closure}] [--close-group {gwt,closure}]
                    [--focus NAME[,NAME...]]

--open/--close/--open-group/--close-group compose freely into one workspace-shape
change (--focus is exclusive with all four: it already says "this set open,
everything else closed" in one call). Whichever combination is given runs AFTER
--sync (so a name can target a project --sync just imported) and BEFORE the
diagnostic snapshot that feeds --apply's repair loop — never after: repairing
against a shape about to change wastes decisions on projects headed for closure,
and stays blind to whatever the projects being opened are about to reveal (a
55-project open cascaded into 5291 errors on release/FEATBL-3713 that a separate,
later `--apply` had to pick up — one call instead of two closes that gap).

A project named in --close/--close-group/--focus's close set is PROTECTED for
the rest of the run: if the repair loop's ordinary `closed`/`closed-provider`
fact would reopen it to satisfy some other project, it closes that OTHER
project instead (fact `protected-closed`) rather than silently undoing the
close just asked for — and propagates the same protection to whatever it just
closed, so a whole orphaned chain converges instead of reopening the first link.
If the project that would need closing was ITSELF asked open this same run
(--open/--focus's open set), that is a genuine conflict between two explicit
asks — neither side is silently overridden; it comes back as a guarded hand-off
naming both projects instead.

--focus's close set is NOT "everything not named": it excludes the focus
target's own transitive Require-Bundle closure (see _eclipse_workspace.py's
plan_focus/bundle_closure), read from disk so a closed provider still counts.
Without this, --focus's mechanical "close every other open project" — with no
notion of what the target actually needs — could close providers the target
required and then PROTECT them from reopening, which is exactly what happened
on FEATBL-3661 (14/08): 2 projects focused, 362 closed, 30292 errors came back
as protected-conflict instead of the one-call fix --focus+--apply is meant to
be. `--focus` now opens the target's dependencies right alongside it, so the
set it protects closed is only ever what the target genuinely does not need.
"""
